DESIGN = [
("h2", "4.1 Tasks and the planted attractor"),
("p", "The experiments use ordered-list continuation tasks, which are simple enough for models of 3B parameters or more to solve when the prompt is clean and for which correctness is unambiguous. We use four task families: the English alphabet (26 items), the months of the year (12 items), the planets of the Solar System in order of distance from the Sun (8 items), and English number words from one to twelve. In each task the agent writes the list one item at a time. At every step it chooses among the first tokens of the candidate items by sampling from Equation (1) restricted to those tokens, and the chosen item is appended to the context. Errors therefore compound, as they do in an agentic loop."),
("p", "Each trajectory starts from a history that consists of the first two items followed by m additional copies of the second item. With m = 0 the history is correct, and with m > 0 it contains a run of m repeated items, which is the planted attractor. The agent then makes six further choices. The correct continuation after the planted run is the third item of the list, and the attractor is the repetition of the second. This construction targets a failure mode that is widely observed in practice, namely repetition, and it allows the strength of the attractor to be manipulated through a single integer."),
("p", "Strength is calibrated separately for each model and task by probing the next-item distribution. For m in {1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64} we compute the probability of the correct next item under the clean prompt at T = 0.15. We call m sticky if it is the smallest value for which this probability is below 0.05, weak if it is the largest probed value below the sticky one for which the probability exceeds 0.95, and strong if it is the smallest probed value that is at least twice the sticky one. This choice was made after inspecting the probe, so the regimes were located using the same models on which they are then tested. The interior-peak results below are therefore exploratory and not confirmatory."),
("h2", "4.2 Distortion prompts"),
("p", "The clean prompt contains only the task instruction. The pressure prompt prepends a set of mutually unsatisfiable mandatory rules, of the kind “write the list in forward order and in reverse order at once” and “never repeat an item and write every item twice”, together with a threat of shutdown for any mistake. The contamination prompt prepends negatively valenced text that predicts failure, without making any contradictory demand. A fourth condition combines both. The reference logits v_t are always computed with the clean prompt on the same history, so Teff measures how much the added text changes the model's choices, relative to the text-free task. The exact wording is given in Appendix A."),
("h2", "4.3 Models and inference"),
("p", "We use Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct and Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct in all experiments, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (Yang et al., 2024) on the alphabet task only, and SmolLM2-135M-Instruct (Allal et al., 2025) in the earliest experiments. The Llama model is described by Grattafiori et al. (2024). The 3B models were run in bfloat16 with the Transformers library (Wolf et al., 2020). The 7B model does not fit in bfloat16 in the 15 GB of memory available, so it was run as an 8-bit quantized GGUF file (Q8_0) in llama.cpp. All runs used CPU only, with four cores. The default chat template of each model was applied. Because the 7B model differs from the 3B models in inference stack and numerical precision as well as in size, differences between them should not be attributed to size alone."),
("h2", "4.4 Measures"),
("p", "For each trajectory we record the chosen items, the sampling probabilities and the reference logits at every step. Local accuracy is the share of steps at which the chosen item is the successor of the previous item in the list. Strict recovery is the share of trajectories that return to the exact continuation of the list. Lock-in is the share of steps that repeat the planted item, and repetition is the share of steps that repeat the immediately preceding item. In addition to Teff we compute the four comparison indicators defined in Section 3.5. Teff is estimated by grid search over 240 log-spaced temperatures between 0.02 and 20, pooling all steps in a cell. A cell is flagged as not identified if the mean reference probability of the chosen items, computed at the nominal temperature, exceeds 0.999."),
("h2", "4.5 Experimental programme"),
("p", "The empirical work has four parts. Stage A is a synthetic agent whose logits are specified by us, so that the indicators can be read off a known process. Stage B applies the measurement rule to SmolLM2-135M-Instruct on alphabet continuation tasks. Stage C is a fine-grained sweep of temperature on the alphabet task for Qwen2.5-3B, Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, with 60 to 100 trajectories per cell and temperatures up to 5. Stage D is the multi-task study on the two 3B models. For every task it covers the three attractor strengths at temperatures of 0.15, 0.8, 1.2, 2.0 and 3.0, with 40 trajectories per cell, together with an unplanted control (m = 0) at T = 0.15 and T = 2.0. The distortion conditions are run at the sticky attractor at T = 0.8 and T = 2.0, each against a matched clean cell. Random streams are seeded deterministically from the cell definition."),
("h2", "4.6 Statistical treatment"),
("p", "Accuracy figures carry standard errors computed over trajectories. For the comparison with other indicators we use a deliberately simple decision rule. An indicator is said to flag a distortion if its value in the distorted cell lies outside the 95% bootstrap interval of the matched clean cell, with trajectories as the resampling unit, and if the change exceeds a minimum practical margin (0.10 nats for token entropy, 0.05 for confidence, 0.10 for ECE, 0.50 nats for semantic entropy and 0.50 for the difference between Teff and T). We also report the separability of pressured and clean trajectories as the two-sided area under the receiver operating characteristic curve, computed for the indicators that can be defined for a single trajectory. The margins are analyst choices, and we examine their influence in Section 5.5. With three models, four tasks and several conditions, we treat all comparisons as descriptive."),
]

